Durarara!! AU
Chapter 36 – Part 1
Ikebukuro. Night. A red Softail taking a corner like it had been born in the lane.
Curtis rode with the oni seal on and the city smeared into lights. The meeting ping was still a live coal in his pocket. Mika’s name sat behind his teeth. Two keys were on a table he was trying not to think about.
He sneezed.
The helmet made it ridiculous.
“Achoo—” A sniff. A mutter into the voice module, dry as ever. “Oh dear. Someone’s talking about me.”
He said it like a joke you tell a brother who is not on the back of the bike.
The Softail rolled on.
Somewhere in Los Angeles, four people were packing a wrong map and a right grief.
Somewhere in a furnished flat, two girls were sitting with a red cap and a song they had killed.
Curtis Nightstar wiped the visor with the side of a glove, revved once, and aimed the Harley at a crowd that did not know his real name.
On a red Softail in a city that collected rumors, the man in the demon helmet
sneezed and blamed the living. He was not wrong. He just had the wrong continent.
The visor lit up.
A name. A number. A small clean panel floating on the inside of the seal like the helmet had been waiting to earn its price.
Curtis huffed, pleased in spite of the night.
“Oh. This is sweet. Worth the money.”
He killed the voice changer with a tap at the jaw and answered.
“Mika.”
A shaky inhale on the other end. City noise behind her. A van engine idling. Someone muttering in Japanese about maps.
“You— you said if I called.” Her English was careful. The reconstructed face did not come through the speaker. Only the girl. “I called.”
“I know. I’m on the bike. Talk to me.” He took a quieter street without thinking, Softail dropping a gear. “Where are you. Who’s with you.”
A pause. Then, smaller:
“People. A van. They said they wouldn’t hurt me. They said the Dollars told them to keep me moving until the meeting.” Another swallow. “There is a man with kind eyes who looks tired. A driver who complains. Two people in the back who talk like a comic book.”
Kadota. Togusa. Walker. Erika.
Curtis’s mouth ticked.
“You’re safe enough,” he said. Not a guess. A read. “Those four don’t traffic. They collect strays and argue about anime. Stay in the van. Don’t get out for anyone who smiles too hard.”
Mika’s voice thinned.
“The leader told them.” She said leader like a weather system. “They don’t say a name. They just say the Dollars asked. I don’t know if that is good.”
Curtis did not give her Mikado.
He did not give the van a founder.
“It’s a crowd with a phone tree,” he said. “Some of it is decent. Some of it isn’t. The people in that van are the decent part. That’s why I can hear you and not lose my mind on this light.”
From her end, Erika, distant, delighted:
“She has a friend on the phone! Is it the black rider? Is it a secret boyfriend? Walker, this is so—”
“Erika.” Kadota, warning.
Mika almost laughed. It broke.
“They are loud.”
“Yeah.” Curtis’s visor painted the next turn. “Loud is better than quiet rooms. You tell tired-eyes I said keep her in the van until I roll up. You don’t have to use my name. Say the guy from the sidewalk. He’ll know.”
“The sidewalk,” Mika whispered. Like the word was a building.
“I’m coming,” Curtis said. No poetry. A job. “You did right calling. Sit. Breathe. If anyone in a lab coat shows up, you scream first and explain later.”
“Okay.”
He did not say Anri knows. He did not say two girls have your name on a couch. He did not say I just sneezed because California is talking.
“Mika.”
“Yes.”
“You’re not a rumor tonight. You’re a person in a van. I’m two songs away.”
He hung up.
The voice changer stayed off for half a block, like his own mouth needed the air.
Then the seal went back to demon, and the Softail aimed at a kind-eyed man who did not know the Dollars had a first-year for a king.
On a HUD that had earned its price, Mika Harima called the sidewalk.
Curtis Nightstar killed the filter so she would hear a person.
The van gang had their stray. The leader still had no name in that vehicle. Ikebukuro kept collecting them.
The call had already clicked dead. Mika still had the phone to her ear like the sidewalk might come back out of it.
“Curtis-kun…”
The van did not miss it.
Erika sat forward so hard the bench complained.
“Did you just say Curtis?!” Delight and alarm in the same breath. “The one Dotachin won’t stop calling a problem?”
Kadota’s eyes found the rearview like the name had rear-ended him.
Walker’s grin arrived first, unhelpful.
“No way. Problem-kun? Tall? Braids? Looks like he walked out of a seinen and refused to pay the extra?”
Togusa groaned into the wheel.
“Why is it always the tall ones.”
Mika lowered the phone. The reconstructed face did not know their jokes. The girl did.
“He told me to stay in the van,” she said. Careful. “He said you were loud. And decent. He said to tell tired-eyes—” a glance at Kadota, “—keep me here until he rolls up. He said I don’t have to use his name. I already did. I’m sorry.”
Erika clasped her hands.
“She has a kun. Dotachin. She has a kun for the problem.”
Kadota did not take the bait. His voice stayed level, the way it did when a night was about to grow a second night.
“…How do you know Curtis.”
Mika looked at the phone.
“He found me. He didn’t take me anywhere I didn’t ask. He told me my name.” A swallow. “He said if I called, he would come. I called.”
Walker whistled, low.
“That’s not a Dollars pickup. That’s a person.”
Kadota’s jaw worked.
Problem. Cartel sentences. Rolling out of a van like a threat and a favor. Friendship as Izaya’s price. A boy who helped Kaz and then made the whole vehicle feel like it had borrowed a wolf.
And now a redhead with no clean story had his number and his honorific.
“He’s coming here,” Kadota said. Not a question.
Mika nodded.
Erika leaned toward her, already composing a character sheet.
“Is he the nice version or the street-fight version? Because we have footage and Dotachin has a lecture—”
“Erika.”
“I’m asking for the plot.”
Togusa flicked the blinker.
“If Problem-kun is rolling up, I want it on a street with cameras and an exit. I’m not getting sandwiched between him and the Headless Rider tonight. My insurance is already crying.”
Kadota looked at Mika again.
Kind eyes. Tired. Doing the math he always did too late.
“He called us decent,” he muttered. Almost offended. Almost not. “After I spent a whole job calling him a problem.”
Walker shrugged.
“Both can be true. That’s why it’s interesting.”
Mika held the phone in both hands.
“He said I was a person. Not a rumor.” Her voice thinned. “Please don’t make him a problem before he gets here. He is the first thing that felt like a sidewalk instead of a room.”
Erika’s excitement dipped, just a hair, into something that understood a girl in a van.
Kadota exhaled through his nose.
“We keep her in the van,” he said. “We wait. If Curtis shows, we don’t start a fight in my friend’s car. And nobody—” a look at Erika and Walker, “—asks him if he’s the masked guy from the internet in front of the girl.”
Walker raised both hands. “I would never.”
Togusa: “He would.”
The van idled in Ikebukuro traffic, one more stray in the back, a name they already feared a little sitting in the air like weather.
On a bench seat between comic-book noise and a tired captain,
Mika Harima said Curtis-kun and the van learned the sidewalk had a number.
Kyouhei Kadota heard problem come home to roost.
Erika Karisawa got a plot.
Walker Yumasaki got a grin.
Saburo Togusa wanted an exit.
The Softail was already two songs away.
The meeting had not started yet.
Celty had left Mikado on a sidewalk with Izaya’s smile still hanging in the air and taken Shooter home because even a dullahan needed a door that was hers for ten minutes.
The apartment smelled like nothing. It always smelled like nothing to her. That was the problem. She had already photographed the nothing. She already had a boy across the ward who would not laugh a stain into coffee.
Shinra was in the living room like a man who had been rehearsing.
Glasses. Coat still on. That bright, certain face he wore when he had decided a feeling was a fact.
“Welcome back, Celty.” Soft. Fond. Completely on the wrong night. “I know the city’s loud. I know the head is still out there. I just— can we talk before you put the helmet back on and disappear into another job?”
She set the helmet on the table. Typed.
Make it quick. There is a meeting.
Shinra laughed like she had told a joke.
“There’s always a meeting. There’s always a courier run. There’s always a reason to put the city first.” He stepped closer. Not grabbing. Worse. Gentle. “I’ve been thinking. About the twenty years. About the memories you made here that you didn’t have when you arrived. About whether the head is the only thing that makes a life.”
Black smoke ticked once above her collar.
Curtis’s kitchen. A fortune slip she had stolen from Mei’s reach. If I get my head back, you’re coming with me sitting in a private channel she had never put in Shinra’s mouth. A desk that had been wiped. A kit. A yes she had given a foreigner to search this house.
Shinra kept going, because Shinra always kept going.
“If you never found it— if this is all you get— I don’t think it was a waste. The years you lived here were well spent. Use what you’ve learned going forward and you’ll see your life has purpose here.” He smiled like a proposal could be a thesis defense. “Celty. Will you marry me?”
The PDA sat in her glove.
The apartment waited.
In her head — the place only one person in Ikebukuro could hear, and he was on a Softail sneezing at California — the answer arrived in pieces.
He wants me to stay as I am.
He says it is love.
Sometimes it feels like a cage with kind wallpaper.
He is proposing on a night I left a first-year with an informant.
He is proposing in a room I cannot smell.
He is proposing like Mei does not exist and Curtis does not exist and the cookie did not name a living half.
She typed because her hands needed a job.
You are asking me this now.
“I know the timing is selfish.” He did not look ashamed enough. “I just don’t want you to think the only future I see for you is a search. I see you. Head or not. Monster or not. I have always seen you.”
Always.
The word landed next to a kitchen laugh she had walked in on. Next to Celty can’t smell, it’s fine. Next to a foreigner who had taken his shirt off because she was tired of pieces.
Celty’s smoke rose, thin and uneven.
She did not write no.
She did not write yes.
She did not write I already told another man I love him where he cannot hear it.
I have to go. The meeting.
Shinra’s face flickered. Not collapse. The particular hurt of a man who had offered destiny and received a schedule.
“Celty—”
I heard you. The stylus hesitated. I need the city tonight. Not a ring.
She took the helmet.
Shooter woke in the hall like a horse that had felt the split in her.
Shinra stood in the clean apartment with a proposal still open and a roommate who could not smell what he had been doing in it.
Celty rode back toward Sunshine, toward a redhead who was wearing a stolen face, toward a crowd that was about to learn what she was.
The question sat in the visor with her.
Marry the cage.
Or keep the door that had offered to help her leave and still be glad.
She did not answer it on the way. Ikebukuro would not wait for a dullahan to pick a house.
Sometime earlier, Shinra Kishitani asked for a hand that was already holding another city.
Celty Sturluson put the helmet on and chose the meeting.
The ring stayed in the air. Mei was not in the room.
Curtis Nightstar was two songs from a van and did not know a proposal had just followed him into the night.
The door had barely shut on Shooter’s engine when the other one opened.
Mei came in like she lived there. Pink hair. Coat half off. The easy walk of someone who had never needed a key explained to her.
She took in the helmet’s empty place on the table. Shinra still standing in the middle of the room with his proposal hanging off him.
“Aww. Too bad.” Bright. Not kind. “Looks like a meeting is more important.”
Shinra adjusted his glasses. The smirk arrived on schedule, the one he used when a result had not gone his way and he had already rewritten it as pending.
“That’s fine. She just needs more time to decide.”
Mei’s mouth pulled.
“Time.” She set her bag on the chair Celty could not smell. “You asked a headless woman to marry you on a courier night and she chose a crowd. That’s not time, Shinra. That’s an answer wearing a helmet.”
He laughed under it.
“Celty processes in motion. She always has. Twenty years of motion. She heard me. She said she heard me. That’s more than most people get from her on a first ask.”
Mei looked at him for a long second. Scientist. Friend. The other woman in a house that pretended it was only one thing.
“You’re smiling,” she said. “She rode away from a ring and you’re smiling.”
“I’m optimistic.” He pushed the glasses up again, brighter, worse. “She didn’t say no. She said the city. The city ends. She comes home. We finish the conversation.”
Mei hummed, already bored of his thesis and already close enough that the clean air in the apartment was a joke only one of them could smell.
“And if the city is a person now,” she said, almost idle. “If the meeting isn’t the only thing that got more important.”
Shinra’s smirk held.
“Celty loves me. That isn’t a hypothesis. That’s twenty years of data.”
Mei smiled with her teeth.
“Data,” she echoed. “Sure.”
She did not tell him about a foreigner with braids. She did not tell him about a race she had not been invited to. She did not tell him that more time was the sentence men used when a woman had already left the room.
“Well.” A shrug. “If she needs time, we have a night. The meeting will keep her busy. You can stop standing in the hallway like a rejected rom-com.”
Shinra’s laugh came back, lighter.
“I’m not rejected. I’m pending.”
Mei walked past him toward the office, toward the desk that had already been wiped once.
Pending.
Celty was already in the wind.
Curtis was already answering a van.
Ikebukuro did not care which word Shinra put on a no that had worn a helmet out the door.
In an apartment that still could not hold a smell,
Mei walked in on a ring that had not landed.
Shinra Kishitani called it time.
Mei called it a meeting.
Both of them were wrong in different directions. The dullahan was already gone.
The visor threw a map pin into the middle of a street that had no business hosting a congregation.
Curtis stared at it.
“A Dollars meeting. In the middle of a street.” He exhaled through his teeth. “I have to wonder what Mikado’s up to.”
He called him.
No voice changer. No oni filter. Just the Softail idling under a light and a first-year who had built a color gang without a color.
“Mikado. What are you doing. What’s with the meeting.”
A small inhale. City noise. The particular tightness of a boy standing somewhere public with a plan that had already left his hands.
“Curtis-san.” Careful. The honorific he used when he wanted to sound older than the jealousy. “You got the ping.”
“I got a cage with a time stamp. Talk.”
Mikado talked.
Yagiri Pharmaceuticals. Namie. The redhead everyone wanted and nobody had named correctly. He had asked her to meet him on Sunshine 60 — Tokyu Hands, the open street, the worst place for a quiet crime. The offer was a location in exchange for the truth about Mika Harima. Not a rumor. An admission. Then they turn themselves in.
“She won’t,” Curtis said.
“I know.” Mikado’s voice thinned and then steeled. “That’s why the meeting is the same place. Same minute. I sent the notice to every member. When her people move, I send one more message. Every phone in the crowd rings. She looks up and the street is the Dollars. Not a color. Not a boss on a throne. Just… people. Staring. I walk into them and I’m gone.”
Curtis watched a light change. Did not roll yet.
“You’re using a mob as a door.”
“I’m using a joke I made on the internet to stop a company from eating a girl.” A breath. “Kadota has her. I asked him to take her to the Black Rider on the roof. Celty should see her. I thought— if the head and the girl are in the same night, the lie breaks in public instead of in a lab.”
Curtis’s jaw worked.
He already had Mika in his ear. He already had Anri on a couch with her name. He already knew the van was loud and decent. Mikado did not need to hear any of that like a scoreboard.
“Namie doesn’t turn herself in because a first-year asks nicely in front of a department store,” Curtis said. “Seiji doesn’t wait his turn. Izaya doesn’t attend anything he can’t sell later. You’re about to make the Dollars real. After tonight they stop being bored kids on a site. Every idiot with a grudge is going to wear your name.”
Silence. Then, smaller, and prouder for it:
“I know. Izaya said evolution. Masaomi said stay out. I’m doing it anyway. Mika was my classmate. Anri asked me where she went. I hid a girl in my apartment and then I lost her. This is me putting the cards on the table.”
Curtis almost smiled. It did not reach kind.
“You hid a girl and built a street around the consequence. That’s not cards. That’s a stage.” The visor painted Sunshine. “I’m not your member. I’m not standing in your ringtone choir so you can feel like a founder. I’m rolling to the van because the girl called me. If your plan works, great. If it goes sideways, I get her out. Not the brand. Her.”
Mikado swallowed.
“…You’re coming.”
“I’m coming to Mika. Don’t confuse it.” A beat. “And Mikado. If you get a knife in your ribs because you wanted the city to watch you be brave, I’m going to be annoyed. Stay in the crowd when the phones ring. Don’t play hero with Seiji. He’s not a classmate problem.”
“I—” Mikado stopped. Jealousy and gratitude fighting in the same skinny chest. “Understood, Curtis-san.”
Curtis hung up.
The Softail took the green.
A street meeting. A company. A founder who still thought a chorus of ringtones was the same thing as safety.
On a HUD that had already earned its price, Mikado Ryuugamine
told the truth as far as a first-year could throw it.
Trade the girl. Ring the street. Make the Dollars visible.
Curtis Nightstar aimed a Harley at the van instead of the throne.
Sunshine 60 was about to learn what a color without a color looked like in person.
The call with Mikado died.
Curtis stared at the pin on Sunshine 60 like it had grown teeth.
“Fuck all kinds of duck.”
The visor did not care. The Softail idled. The alley from Mikado’s building sat down in his chest all over again — two coats, a transferred number, an ankle, a sentence he had not wanted.
There’s a hit on her. Someone wants her dead. It’s happening tonight.
Namie.
The woman who had said maybe in a clean car. The last name on a company that ate girls and called it research. The person he had told two trash coats they did not get to forget.
And now Mikado had invited her onto an open street with a ringtone choir and a first-year’s idea of justice.
Namie was in danger.
Mikado was in danger.
The same night. The same block. A founder who thought a crowd was a shield and a woman who thought a lab coat was armor.
Curtis’s fist tightened on the throttle until the grip complained.
He could peel toward Namie. He could peel toward the van. He could do the stupid thing and try to stand in both fires.
The girl had called him.
The dullahan needed to see the face.
“I’ll oversee the meeting with Celty and Mika,” he told the empty seat behind him, the one Justice was not sitting on. “So she can tell who did the facial reconstruction. Celty hears it from the girl. Not from a rumor. Not from Shinra smiling through it later.”
The oni seal stayed on. The voice changer stayed off in his head.
Priority, ugly and clean:
Van. Roof. Mika. Celty.
Then the street.
Then the hit.
He rolled.
Ikebukuro lights smeared. The Harley took the next corridor toward tired-eyes and comic-book noise and a redhead who still thought her name might be a monster’s.
Namie Yagiri had a clock.
Mikado Ryuugamine had a stage.
Curtis Nightstar had a meeting to stand in so a headless woman would learn, in the same hour the Dollars became real, that the surgeon in her house had put her face on a classmate.
On a red Softail aimed at Sunshine, Curtis Nightstar cursed the night in full.
A hit. A founder. A girl in a van. He chose the roof first.
The street could wait one song.